---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, POLICY_UPDATED } from "../../components/site-data";
---
<Base
  title="Privacy · Is There a Burn Ban?"
  description="How location lookups, browser storage, usage counts and alert subscriptions work."
  path="/privacy/"
>
  <main id="content" class="page">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">Privacy</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted">Updated {POLICY_UPDATED}.</p>
    <p>
      You can check burn bans without an account. This page explains what the site, location lookup and alert services store.
      There are no ads, no tracking scripts and no cookies of our own, and we don't sell or rent anything about you.
    </p>

    <h2 id="location">Your location</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        When you tap <em>Use my location</em>, your browser asks you first. If you allow it, the page rounds the
        position to four decimal places (about 10 meters) and sends it to our status lookup to find your county.
        A ZIP code you type is sent the same way.
      </li>
      <li>
        The county lookup sends coordinates rounded to three decimals to the US Census geocoder. Cloudflare can cache the county match
        keyed by those rounded coordinates for up to 30 days.
      </li>
      <li>
        Your browser remembers the chosen county and the last report separately in local storage. It checks again on your next visit
        and may show a dated last-known report if the check fails. Clear this site's browser data to remove both.
      </li>
      <li>The home page asks for permission to use your location. API clients can request an approximate IP-based county match from Cloudflare.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="email">Email alerts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If you sign up on the site or through an assistant, we store your address, the counties you picked, a subscription token
        and confirmation and unsubscribe state.
      </li>
      <li>
        When email delivery is enabled, we send a confirmation email first. Alerts begin after you confirm.
      </li>
      <li>
        Alert emails include an unsubscribe link. It disables the subscription tied to that link. Messages already queued may still arrive.
        Contact us to delete your signup data or remove all subscriptions for an address.
      </li>
      <li>When enabled, Resend receives your email address and the message to handle delivery.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="push">Push alerts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If you turn on alerts, your browser gives us a push subscription with a web address at your browser
        maker's push service (Google, Apple, Mozilla or Microsoft) plus encryption keys. We store that and the counties you
        picked. That registration is separate from email subscriptions.
      </li>
      <li>Removing a push subscription through our unsubscribe endpoint removes that device registration and the counties attached to it. Push services can also expire subscriptions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="logs">Delivery records and usage counts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        For each alert we send, we log the county, the result and a masked target (for example the first letter of an email
        address), so we can fix delivery problems.
      </li>
      <li>
        We count requests to our API and MCP server: which endpoint or tool, the response status, the country, and a broad
        client type (browser, bot, AI agent or script). Our structured counts exclude IP addresses, full browser details and query values.
      </li>
      <li>
        The site runs on Cloudflare. Like any web host, Cloudflare handles IP addresses to deliver pages and block abuse.
        We don't keep request logs with IP addresses ourselves.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="sharing">Sharing</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare, the US Census geocoder, Resend and browser push providers handle the information described above to run lookups and deliver alerts. We may disclose information where required by law.
    </p>

    <h2 id="contact">Questions or deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. We'll delete your email signup or answer questions about
      your data. See also the <a href="/terms/">terms</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
